  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and fairly muggy through the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nSimilar temperatures to today expected on Thursday, then \nslightly less hot Friday through Sunday in the north, but just \nabout as hot for the Bangor region and Downeast. Thursday will \nbe muggier than today with dewpoints increasing slowly. \n\nFriday will feel muggier with dewpoints nearing 70. At some point, \nwe could approach heat advisory criteria of heat indices of 95, most \nlikely on Friday and in the Bangor region. In addition nighttime \nlows are quite mild from Thursday night into the weekend. There will \nbe a bit of relief from the heat near the coast mainly as afternoon \nsea breezes develop, though any of the days could get quite hot \nbefore the sea breeze kicks in.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered showers and thunderstorms late this \nweek. Some severe storms are possible. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system approaches from the northwest, moving into\nNew Brunswick. Associated cold front is continuing to trend\nslower, as it approaches Friday night, through Maine by \nSaturday evening. General trends is pointing towards the heavier\nrainfall falling on Saturday. There is plenty of instability \nahead of the cold front. NAM shows around 1,500 - 2,000 J/kg of\nCAPE, good low level lapse rates (roughly 8C/km), and plenty of\nprecipitation for Friday evening and Saturday afternoon into \nevening. Soundings are moist, with classic long and skinny CAPE \nthroughout most of northern and eastern Maine. PWAT values \naround 1.8, with some models hinting at almost 2.0\" on \nSaturday, which is around the 90th percentile, approaching the \n1.9 daytime maximum value Friday evening, and the 1.7\" record \nSaturday. Keeping the threat of thunder inland, as a seabreeze \noff of the cool Gulf of Maine waters should stabilize and keep \ndaytime high temperatures on the lower side along Downeast Maine\ncoastline on both days. Storm motions based on Corfidi vectors \nseems to be moving fast enough to keep flash flooding at bay, \nbut will need to see this evolves as the regional models and \nCAMs pick up on the system and preexisting environment. Bottom \nline up front: Saturday seems to be more favorable for heavier \nrainfall, and more expansive thunderstorms.",
